 with many projects may find it helpful to consider and define their PIR as a separate off-line exercise from any particular project being started.\nGuidance Part 2: Processes for Project Delivery Fourth Edition - April 2020 | 59 3.4.2.3 Who creates PIR? 3.4.2.5 When is the information exchanged to meet the PIR? The appointing party, for example the project client, the asset owner (indirectly through OIR) The activity of requiring information and then or their representative.\ndelivering it is called an information exchange.\n3.4.2.4 What do they include? ISO 19650-2 clause 5.1.2 recommends that the appointing party considers the project plan PIR are derived from the purposes for which of work in establishing the PIR.\nThis enables the appointing party requires information.\nkey decision points and associated activities These can include: such as information exchange to be anchored \u2022 Relevant OIR such as corporate key against a defined plan, for example the RIBA performance indicators (KPIs) plan of work.\n\u2022 Project business case for example, financial Key decision points are when the appointing information to establish value for money party and other stakeholders make informed and affordability decisions about the project such as, whether \u2022 Strategic brief for example, strategic or not it is financially viable to proceed to the programme to establish opening date for a next stage or decisions about appointment of school the project team.\nThese decisions are made \u2022 Project stakeholders who require using information received from information information for example, local residents providers.\n\u2022 Project tasks which the appointing party themselves need to carry out for example, A key decision point can be y weeks before completing an application.\nthe end of a work stage or after the beginning of a work stage.\nNote that ISO With the PIR in place the information to be 19650-1 clause 3.2.14 could be read to suggest delivered can then be define