4 I nformation requirements during design and construction project delivery 3.4.1 Introduction ISO 19650-2 requires that the appointing party\u2019s information needs are determined before any delivery team appointments are tendered.\nThere are two types of information requirements to be defined: 1.\nProject information requirements (PIR) 2.\nExchange Information Requirements (EIR) (these will be explained in future guidance).\n3.4.2 Project information requirements (PIR) PIR are explained in ISO 19650-1 clause 5.4 and ISO 19650-2 clause 5.1.2.\nSee Annex A.3 in this guidance for examples of PIR.\nSee also ISO 19650-1 figure 2 for the relationship between the OIR and PIR.\nPIR, like OIR, are high-level and identify what information will be needed for the key decision points determined by the appointing party.\nThere is only one set of PIR per project.\nPIR are partly derived from OIR.\nThey enable understanding of the high-level information the appointing party requires during a design and construction project.\n3.4.2.2 When are PIR defined? Some of the PIR content may have already been defined within OIR that are applicable to design and construction projects.\nFor example, statutory requirements or corporate project delivery policies.\nAny additional PIR should be identified and added to those derived from OIR at the inception of a design and construction project, before the appointments for any consultants or contractors are tendered.\nOrganizations with many projects may find it helpful to consider and define their PIR as a separate off-line exercise from any particular project being started.\nGuidance Part 2: Processes for Project Delivery Fourth Edition - April 2020 | 59 3.4.2.3 Who creates PIR? 3.4.2.5 When is the information exchanged to meet the PIR? The appointing party, for example the project client, the asset owner (indirectly through OIR) The activity of requiring information and then or their representative.\ndelivering it is called an information exchange.\n3.4.2.4 What do they include? ISO 19650-2 clause 5.1.2 recommends that the appointing party considers the project plan PIR are derived from the purposes for which of work in establishing the PIR.\nThis enables the appointing party requires information.\nkey decision points and associated activities These can include: such as information exchange to be anchored \u2022 Relevant OIR such as corporate key against a defined plan, for example the RIBA performance indicators (KPIs) plan of work.\n\u2022 Project business case for example, financial Key decision points are when the appointing information to establish value for money party and other stakeholders make informed and affordability decisions about the project such as, whether \u2022 Strategic brief for example, strategic or not it is financially viable to proceed to the programme to establish opening date for a next stage or decisions about appointment of school the project team.\nThese decisions are made \u2022 Project stakeholders who require using information received from information information for example, local residents providers.\n\u2022 Project tasks which the appointing party themselves need to carry out for example, A key decision point can be y weeks before completing an application.\nthe end of a work stage or after the beginning of a work stage.\nNote that ISO With the PIR in place the information to be 19650-1 clause 3.2.14 could be read to suggest delivered can then be defined more precisely that a key decision point aligns to the end of in EIR (ISO 19650-2 clause 5.2.1).\na work stage but key decisions can be made at any time.\nExamples might include decisions related to tendering or planning permission.\nKey decision points need to be mapped out at the start of the project, see ISO 19650-2 clause 5.1.3.\nInformation delivery milestones are pre- defined points which specify when the required information should be delivered to the appointing party.\nInformation delivery milestones should b